## Vector memory (Qdrant MCP)

Nine tools from the `codebase-indexer` server, all named `cb_indexer_*`: `cb_indexer_find_repo` (semantic search over this project's collection) and `cb_indexer_store_repo` (save a piece of text there so `cb_indexer_find_repo` can return it by meaning), plus `cb_indexer_index_repo`/`cb_indexer_sync_repo`/`cb_indexer_preview_index`/`cb_indexer_get_collection_info`/`cb_indexer_find_in_collection`/`cb_indexer_set_collection_description`/`cb_indexer_list_collections`. Three more tools, `compact_store`/`compact_find`/`compact_prune` for conversation compacts (see below), come from the `qdrant` server.

Prefer `cb_indexer_find_repo` over Grep/Read for conceptual lookups — "where is X handled," "how does Y work," "which files deal with Z." Use Grep directly for exact identifiers, error strings, or known symbols.

Treat results as leads, not facts. If a result's metadata includes file_path and line_number, read from that line with Read (offset=line_number, with a limit) rather than the whole file; a chunk is short, so its text in the hit shows how far it runs. Points indexed before token-window chunking carry `line_range` ("start-end") instead. `cb_indexer_find_repo` returns 10 hits unless you pass `limit`: raise it when what you want is not in the first 10.

Storage convention for cb_indexer_store_repo: store short text, not raw file dumps. When the text is about a file, add file_path + line_number metadata; when it comes from elsewhere, add source/url; otherwise metadata is optional.

Run cb_indexer_sync_repo at the start of a session (or after a batch of edits) to keep the index current — it only re-embeds files that changed, so it's cheap to run often. Use cb_indexer_index_repo with reset=true only for first-time indexing or after changing chunking/embedding settings. Call cb_indexer_get_collection_info if cb_indexer_find_repo comes back empty, to check whether the collection actually has data.

Dedup within a session: if the same conceptual question resurfaces, reuse the earlier cb_indexer_find_repo result instead of re-querying.

**Conversation compacts:** `compact_store` saves a summary of the current conversation into this project's own compact collection, compressing it on the local model first when LM Studio is available and storing it as given when it is not. `compact_find` lists a project's saved compacts newest first, or searches them when given a `query`. `compact_prune` deletes old compacts by `keep_last_n` or `older_than_days`, and only reports what it would delete unless you pass `dry_run=False`. `/cr-compact` and `/cr-resume` call `compact_store` and `compact_find` for you, so you rarely call them by hand. Never use them for notes about the code: that is what `cb_indexer_store_repo`/`cb_indexer_find_repo` are for, and compacts stored there would be buried under code chunks.

**Cross-repo lookups:** `cb_indexer_find_repo` only ever searches THIS project's own collection. When a question genuinely requires another already-indexed repo's codebase — e.g. working in a frontend repo but needing to understand how the backend it calls actually implements an endpoint — use `cb_indexer_find_in_collection(query, collection)` instead. If the user names an exact collection directly in their prompt, pass it through as given rather than guessing. Call `cb_indexer_list_collections` first if you don't know the other repo's exact collection name. Don't reach for this for questions answerable from this project's own collection; it's specifically for when the answer lives in a different repo's code. `cb_indexer_set_collection_description(collection, description)` sets the one-line hint `cb_indexer_list_collections` shows for a collection; call it only when the user asks, since this project's own hint normally comes from `COLLECTION_DESCRIPTION` in `.mcp.json`.

## Durable memory (memory-bank MCP)

Three tools: `remember`, `recall`, `forget` — a durable, cross-session knowledge store, separate from `cb_indexer_store_repo`/`cb_indexer_find_repo` above (those work on this project's own collection, whatever you and the user choose to put in it). memory-bank is for lessons, decisions, and corrections meant to survive a fresh session, and can span every project on this machine.

Call `recall` before finalizing a non-trivial change — a past correction, root cause, or design rationale relevant to the current task may already be stored. It defaults to this project's own memories plus anything tagged general (applies everywhere) and, if this project follows other repos, what they marked shared; only pass `repo=<name>` or `all_repos=True` when you genuinely need a different or broader scope than that.

Offer to `remember` something after resolving a real correction, a non-obvious root cause, or a design decision with real rationale behind it — not every exchange. Ask first ("Is this something you'd like to remember for next time?") rather than storing automatically. Write `summary` the way a future query would be phrased (it's the only text actually searched); put the full verbatim detail in `description`.

Set `general=True` on `remember` ONLY when the user explicitly signals this applies across every project — phrases like "remember this globally," "this applies to every project," "remember this across all repos." Default to `False` (scoped to this project) otherwise; don't infer `general=True` from the content alone.

Set `shared=True` on `remember` ONLY when the user says the memory is relevant to other repos — phrases like "share this with the related repos," "the repos that follow this one should see it." Default to `False`; don't infer it from the content alone, the same rule as `general`. It offers the memory to every repo that follows this one (the other repos' `MEMORY_BANK_FOLLOW_REPOS` lists this project's `MEMORY_BANK_ID`), and the owner stays this project. It is a relevance filter, not privacy: `recall(repo=...)` and `all_repos=True` still return any memory, shared or not. It is not valid together with `general=True`, and like `weight` it can only be changed by `forget`-ing the point and `remember`-ing it again. Following is configuration in `.mcp.json` (`MEMORY_BANK_FOLLOW_REPOS`, or `setup_project.py init --memory-bank-follow`) and takes effect when the server next starts; no tool changes it. A `notes` entry in a `recall` result reports a followed id with no memories or an ignored list entry, so mention it to the user.

`remember` also takes an optional, FINITE, NONNEGATIVE `weight` (default `1.0`, a true no-op; a negative, infinite, or NaN value is rejected) that `recall` uses to re-rank hits instead of raw similarity alone — use `>1.0` to boost a memory you know is especially trustworthy, or `0.0` to de-emphasize a stale/superseded one without deleting it (it scales relevance proportionally — `effective_score = max(0, similarity) * weight` — so it boosts among comparably-relevant hits and never lifts an irrelevant memory). Don't set it away from the default without a real reason; there's no separate "update weight" tool, so changing it later means `forget`-ing the point and `remember`-ing it again.

`forget` needs explicit confirmation from the user before calling with `confirm=True` — never set it automatically. Show what would be deleted (a `recall` result, or a `wipe_all` dry-run count) and get the user's go-ahead first.

A fourth tool, `transfer_memories(source_collection, dry_run=True)`, copies memories from a legacy Qdrant collection into memory-bank. Use it only when the user asks for a migration and names the source collection. Run it with the default `dry_run=True` first, show the user the report, and pass `dry_run=False` only after they agree. It only migrates points tagged with this project or `general`, and never deletes the source.

A fifth tool, `copy_memories(source_repo, kind="all", dry_run=True)`, copies another project's memories (all, or one `kind`) into THIS project. Use it only when the user asks for it and names the exact `source_repo` (the other project's `MEMORY_BANK_ID`; don't guess one). It always writes under this project's own tag, so to copy memories into another project, run it from a session in that project. Run it with the default `dry_run=True` first, show the user the report (each memory's kind and summary, plus the counts), and pass `dry_run=False` only after they agree; there is no per-memory selection, so to leave some out, re-run with a single `kind`. `general` is not a valid source, since every project already recalls general memories.

A sixth tool, `list_memory_banks()`, is read-only: it returns the shared collection's name and, per repo, how many memories it has and whether it is "sharable" (has at least one `shared` memory, which is what a follower would receive). It returns ids and counts, never memory text. Call it when the user wants to know which repo ids exist or what following one would add, instead of asking them to remember ids; to look inside a repo, use `recall(repo=<id>, query=...)`. It does not change anything and does not replace the user naming the repo to follow or to copy from.

**memory-bank takes priority over Claude Code's own local/auto memory system (the files under `~/.claude/projects/.../memory/`) for anything both could hold.** When a finding is a lesson, a correction, or a design decision with rationale — the kind of thing the local memory system's own "feedback"/"project" categories describe — write it via `remember`, not to a local memory file, so there's one source of truth instead of two copies that can drift apart. Reserve the local memory system for what `remember` structurally can't do: things that need to be loaded automatically into every session's context with no explicit retrieval step (user profile/collaboration preferences), and cross-tool reference pointers. If genuinely unsure which one fits, prefer `remember`.

## Local compression (LM Studio MCP)

Five tools from the `local-compress` server: `compress_file`, `compress_command_output`, `fetch_url` (use these), `compress_text` (narrow case only — see below), and `list_local_models` (a status check). The savings and metrics tools are not on this server: they live on the opt-in `analytics` server (see the Analytics section, when present).

Prefer `compress_command_output` over Bash when a command is expected to produce large, mostly-mechanical output — test suites, builds, verbose linters — and you want to know the gist or whether it failed, not read the full log. Prefer `compress_file` over Read for large mechanical files already on disk (saved logs, big diffs) under the same condition. Prefer `fetch_url` over WebFetch when you want the page reviewed and summarized by the local model instead of by WebFetch's own Anthropic-hosted extraction step — `fetch_url` lets you steer what to extract via `focus`, and does the fetch, extraction, and summarization entirely server-side/locally. Do NOT use `fetch_url` for pages needing auth, JavaScript rendering, or session handling — use WebFetch for those. All three run/read/fetch server-side, so the raw content never enters your context — only the summary does. That's what makes these save tokens; reading the output yourself first and then trying to compress it does not.

POSITIONAL asks (the lead/intro section, the first paragraph, the first N lines, an abstract) work through `focus` alone — describe the position in plain language (e.g. `focus="summarize the lead section"`), the same way you'd describe a topic-based ask. Recognized positional phrasings are auto-truncated to the document's actual beginning before anything else happens, deterministically — this isn't left to the model's judgment, because testing showed even a classifier explicitly told each chunk's position wasn't reliably followed by every local model (one real test still returned a digest of the article's Techniques section mislabeled as "the lead section"). Topic-based asks (a specific fact, a named concept) go through the position-aware classifier and work well, including correctly returning an explicit not-found error rather than fabricating something when a topic isn't actually present — verified against a real page and a real local model. `max_chars` is available as an explicit override on any of the three if you want a specific size rather than the automatic default.

Do NOT use either for source code you intend to edit — read that directly with Read so you have exact, unmodified content to work from. Do NOT use them when you need the exact output verbatim (something you're about to parse, a diff you intend to apply, an exact error string you'll grep for next) — a summary is for deciding where to look next, not a substitute for precise text. If a summary reveals something needing exact follow-up (a line number, an exact error string), read that specific file or re-run a narrower command directly afterward rather than trusting the summary as authoritative.

compress_text does not save tokens if you already had to read something into context to call it — only use it for content you legitimately already hold (e.g. your own long draft), not as a way to compress something you just read or ran.

If list_local_models reports LM Studio isn't reachable or no model is loaded, fall back to reading/running things directly rather than blocking on it.

## Analytics (opt-in MCP server)

Include this section only if the project's `.mcp.json` has an `analytics` server. `init --track-savings` writes that block; without the flag it is not written, and then these tools do not exist: calling one is an unknown-tool error, **not** an empty result or "no data yet". Do not conclude savings or metrics are zero because a call failed.

Five read/write tools from the `analytics` server, all backed by local SQLite files (no model, no Qdrant):

- `savings_summary`, `savings_detail`, `savings_trend`: the savings tracker's simple, detail and trend views (characters before and after local compression, per session, per tool and per project). `/cr-savings` calls them. They answer "what is the compression saving me?", and say tracking is off (an explanation, not an error) when `CLAUDE_RUNWAY_TRACK_SAVINGS` is not set.
- `get_metrics(metric_id, view=...)`: reads the shared cross-tool metrics store (views `summary`, `by_event_type`, `trend`, `detail`). `/cr-metrics` calls it. The metric ids with a writer today are `autowork` (per-ticket outcome rows from `/cr-gh-autowork`, including the `ticket_cost` rows `tools/autowork_cost.py record` writes), `memory-bank` (a tally of `remember`/`recall`/`forget` calls), `codebase-reviewer` (one `review` event per review run of the opt-in reviewer server) and `pr-review-rounds` (Claude's cost per pull-request review round; recorded at session end only when `CLAUDE_RUNWAY_CAPTURE_PR_ROUNDS` is on in the shell). Any other id returns "no events recorded yet". Savings are a separate store: read them through the savings tools, not `get_metrics`.
- `record_metric(metric_id, event_type, ...)`: appends one event to that metrics store. Only for a caller that has something to log and no CLI for it; routine recording needs no call from you, because savings events are written by the compression server and the hooks, memory-bank counts its own calls, the reviewer logs each review run, and the SessionEnd hook records PR review rounds when that is on.

Reporting CLIs that sit beside these tools and answer what they cannot (run them from the claude-runway checkout; they read files, they never call a model):

- `tools/memory_bank_report.py`: memory-bank usage: the dead-memory ratio and the recall payload trend, per project.
- `tools/autowork_cost.py report`: per-ticket cost of `/cr-gh-autowork` runs from subagent transcripts: tokens by model, cache-read share, tool calls and wall clock.
- `tools/context_breakdown.py`: what a subagent's starting context is made of and why it grows (subcommands `start`, `calibrate`, `growth`, `reads`).
- `tools/report_tool_counts.py`: how many tools each claude-runway MCP server exposes and how big their descriptions are.
- `tools/pr_round_report.py`: Claude's tokens per Copilot review round, per repository, split into the rounds answered with the local `codebase-reviewer` and those answered without it (reads the `pr-review-rounds` events).
- `tools/codebase_reviewer_report.py`: what the `codebase-reviewer`'s runs cost, from its own database.
- `tools/capture_pr_rounds.py`, `tools/backfill_pr_rounds.py` and `tools/record_pr_rounds.py`: write the `pr-review-rounds` events (one session transcript, every transcript of a project that still exists, and one PR from one transcript). The SessionEnd hook runs `capture_pr_rounds.py` for you when `CLAUDE_RUNWAY_CAPTURE_PR_ROUNDS` is on; the other two are for a manual backfill.

## Local review (opt-in MCP server)

Include this section only if the project's `.mcp.json` has a `codebase-reviewer` server. `init --codebase-reviewer` writes that block (`upgrade` does not offer it); without it the tool does not exist, and calling it is an unknown-tool error.

The server runs local models on LM Studio to review a change before Copilot does. Which models, prompts and rubric it uses is entirely the user's: they live in the reviewers file that the block's `REVIEWERS_FILE` points at. That setting is required and has no default, and nothing in this toolkit writes one.

- `list_reviewers(check_models=...)`: validates the reviewers file and shows the settings, the rubric, every reviewer and the stage plan (reasoning, then verifier, then classifier). It runs no review and loads no model. If the file is missing, unreadable or invalid it returns an "Error:" naming the file and every problem: report that to the user, do not guess a path or write a reviewers file yourself. With `check_models=true` it also says which models that must already be loaded are not.

- `review(type, params)`: runs a local review on LM Studio and returns only the findings; the diff never enters your context. One tool for every kind of review: `type` picks it and `params` is a dict of that type's arguments. There are three types. `"reasoning"`: `review(type="reasoning", params={"repo_path": "...", "base_ref": "origin/main"})` has every active reasoning reviewer read the diff `base_ref...head_ref` (`head_ref` defaults to `HEAD`; optional `context_lines` and `chunk_chars`) and returns ALL their findings, grouped by class with duplicates merged. Those findings are NOT verified or classified, so treat them as leads to check, not as confirmed defects. `"verifier"` takes the same params and does more: after the reasoning stage it drops every finding whose quoted code is not in the file (ungrounded), then every active verifier reviewer confirms each remaining finding with a concrete failing input or rejects it, and you get the confirmed findings, the disputed ones (the verifiers disagreed; each one's reasoning is shown), the unchecked ones and one line for each thing dropped. When verifier reviewers are configured, prefer it over `"reasoning"`; with none active its findings are grounded but not verified, and the result says so. `"classifier"` takes the same params and goes one stage further: after verification, every active classifier reviewer checks each finding that was not rejected against every entry of the user's rubric (does it fall under the entry's definition? the answer is the model's probability of yes), the probabilities are averaged, and you get the findings grouped by class with the rubric entry, level and average probability each one took, the findings with no rubric level, and one line for each thing dropped (rejected, ungrounded, or matching no rubric entry). When classifier reviewers are configured, prefer it over `"verifier"`; with none active the findings carry no rubric level, and the result says so. Call it with `params={}` to get a type's keys, defaults and an example. A bad type, key or value, a missing reviewers file, or a model that must be loaded but is not comes back as one "Error:" naming everything wrong: report it to the user rather than guessing around it. A review can take minutes on a large diff.

- `review_report(view, model, target_minutes, this_machine)`: reads the database every review run writes (timing per request, stage and model load) and answers which model combination suits this machine, how long a model takes per request, and which combinations finish a review under a target time. `view` is `"response_times"`, `"review_times"` (default) or `"under_target"` (needs `target_minutes`). It runs no review and calls no model. Pass the pull request number as `pr` in a review's `params` so its timing can be joined to PR metrics.

## Setup and diagnostics (claude-runway skills and CLIs)

Include this section only for a project that has the claude-runway skills installed. Slash commands, each answering one question: `/cr-setup` (configure or upgrade this project's `.mcp.json` and `.claude/settings.json`; needs `CLAUDE_RUNWAY_DIR` exported), `/cr-compact` and `/cr-resume` (save and restore a conversation, see the compacts paragraph above), `/cr-savings` and `/cr-metrics` (read the `analytics` server, see above), `/cr-pr-feedback` (fetch, sort and answer a GitHub PR's review feedback; takes the repository from the checkout), `/cr-doctor` (run the setup check against this project and report whether it passed; needs `CLAUDE_RUNWAY_DIR` exported), `/cr-load-context` (load this project's context at the start of a session: syncs the index in the background, reads the docs this file names, recalls memories, and reports the open pull requests and which tools are live; needs only `git` and the claude-runway MCP tools).

Two CLIs, run from the claude-runway checkout (they read files and never call a model), to diagnose a setup: `tools/doctor.py <project>` reports whether `.mcp.json`'s env blocks (`local-compress`, `qdrant`, `analytics`) and the live shell disagree on the `CLAUDE_RUNWAY_*` variables that must match ("why is compression not happening?"), and also flags a `.claude/settings.json` still running the renamed `compress_bash_output.py` hook, a missing or invalid reviewers file for a project with a `codebase-reviewer` server, and PR-round capture turned on without its SessionEnd hook (it exits 1 on any problem), and `tools/measure_startup_context.py` measures what the MCP servers add to a session's starting context from real transcripts ("what does this toolkit cost me before I type anything?").
